package com.ascent.climb.data.i18n

/**
 * 英文课程要点（47 课 / 215 条）。
 * 作为其他语言的校对基准：其他语言缺条目时回退到这里。
 */
internal val PACK_EN_BRIEF: Map<String, String> = mapOf(

    // ---------- m01 高山环境科学 ----------
    "lesson.m01.brief.1" to "The higher you go, the lower the pressure. The proportion of oxygen in the air barely changes — still about 21% — but the pressure does. Lower pressure means fewer oxygen molecules per breath. That is the root of altitude hypoxia: not less oxygen, but lower partial pressure.",
    "lesson.m01.brief.2" to "Air temperature drops about 6.5 °C per 1,000 m of ascent. Why the snowline sits where it does, and why 5,000 m peaks still hold snow in midsummer, both follow from this rule.",
    "lesson.m01.brief.3" to "Atmospheric pressure falls roughly exponentially with altitude. At 5,000 m it is about half of sea level — so each breath brings in only half the oxygen molecules.",
    "lesson.m01.brief.4" to "High altitude brings three more often-underestimated factors: markedly stronger UV (about 10–12% more per 1,000 m), extremely dry air, and intense reflection off snow.",
    "lesson.m01.brief.5" to "The gear, pacing and altitude-illness prevention covered later all derive from these physical rules.",

    // ---------- m02 体能准备与营养 ----------
    "lesson.m02.brief.1" to "Alpine climbing rewards long, low-intensity output — aerobic endurance. Explosive power is of little use; under hypoxia you simply cannot summon it.",
    "lesson.m02.brief.2" to "Train progressively and allow full recovery. Taper for the last 2–3 weeks before departure; you should arrive at the mountain fresh, not fatigued.",
    "lesson.m02.brief.3" to "At altitude, eat mostly carbohydrate. Carbs metabolise more efficiently than fat under hypoxia, and the body instinctively craves them. Following that instinct works far better than forcing down high-fat food.",
    "lesson.m02.brief.4" to "Drink small amounts regularly. A practical indicator is pale urine; by the time you feel clearly thirsty you are often already mildly dehydrated.",
    "lesson.m02.brief.5" to "Alcohol and sedatives suppress respiratory drive and worsen nocturnal hypoxia — at altitude they are clearly to be avoided.",

    // ---------- m03 环保与野外礼仪 ----------
    "lesson.m03.brief.1" to "Leave No Trace is the environmental principle promoted in outdoor sports: keep human impact on the mountains as low as possible. Wild places have limited capacity, and popular routes are under constant pressure.",
    "lesson.m03.brief.2" to "Pack out all rubbish — wrappers, food scraps, cigarette ends, and hygiene items such as toilet paper. Before leaving camp, turn around and check the area you used.",
    "lesson.m03.brief.3" to "Human waste should be buried in a cathole or carried out, well away from water sources, camps and trails. This is the most easily overlooked item, yet it directly affects the next party and the safety of the water supply.",
    "lesson.m03.brief.4" to "Store food and rubbish sealed so animals cannot scatter or raid it, and avoid wildlife areas and their sensitive seasons (mating, nursing).",
    "lesson.m03.brief.5" to "Do not damage vegetation, do not remove natural objects, and stay on established trails. New paths cut by foot traffic persist for years and trigger erosion.",

    // ---------- m04 天气与导航基础 ----------
    "lesson.m04.brief.1" to "Read the trend, not the moment. Falling pressure, thickening and lowering cloud, and rising wind are all signs of deterioration. Whether it is sunny right now tells you very little.",
    "lesson.m04.brief.2" to "Clouds are a cheap and effective forecast: cumulus building vertically with an anvil top signals thunderstorms and heavy precipitation; lowering stratus means visibility will drop fast.",
    "lesson.m04.brief.3" to "Common map scales in mountaineering are 1:50,000 and 1:10,000. Closer contour lines mean steeper ground; a closed contour indicates a separate summit.",
    "lesson.m04.brief.4" to "Using a compass: hold it level, keep it away from magnetic objects, never mistake the south end of the needle for north (a 180° error), and correct for local magnetic declination.",
    "lesson.m04.brief.5" to "The core skill is always knowing where you are. Cross-check map, compass and terrain features; do not rely on GPS alone. Electronics run out of power, take on water, and break when dropped.",

    // ---------- m05 目标山峰：入门雪山 ----------
    "lesson.m05.brief.1" to "Introductory snow mountains are mostly around 5,000 m, with walking routes and little technical movement. The real difficulties are acclimatization and weather.",
    "lesson.m05.brief.2" to "Kilimanjaro, 5,895 m (Tanzania, Africa's highest, summit Uhuru Peak): from rainforest to glacier across several climate zones, with no technical gear required.",
    "lesson.m05.brief.3" to "Aotaina, 5,211 m officially (about 4,800 m measured): the \"son of the mountains\" in the Three Ao Snow Mountains, with no technical difficulty and a high success rate — widely regarded as a good first snow peak.",
    "lesson.m05.brief.4" to "Siguniang Da Feng, 5,025 m, and Er Feng, 5,276 m (Xiaojin, Sichuan): the classic introductory pair; the last section of Er Feng needs simple equipment for assistance.",
    "lesson.m05.brief.5" to "When choosing a first peak, compare route maturity, guide and rescue resources, altitude gain rate, and your own fitness and time. The altitude number itself matters less.",

    // ---------- m06 技术攀登入门 ----------
    "lesson.m06.brief.1" to "Technical climbing rests on three systems: arrest (self-arrest with the axe), protection (rope and anchors), and movement (roped team and belay). Together they turn an uncontrolled slide into a controlled risk.",
    "lesson.m06.brief.2" to "Learning technique is not about climbing harder mountains; it is about holding risk at an acceptable level on terrain you cannot avoid. The greater skill is judging when technical gear is not needed at all.",
    "lesson.m06.brief.3" to "Technique must be practised on real ground. Self-arrest needs repeated drills on a gentle, open, soft-snow slope with an instructor belaying, until it becomes a reflex — video and books cannot replace this.",
    "lesson.m06.brief.4" to "The three entry-level pieces are crampons, ice axe and harness. Alone they are useless; they only work when connected into a system with your body and your partner.",
    "lesson.m06.brief.5" to "Technical ability is always subordinate to judgement. Gear and movement are only the means; the decision to go up is what decides survival. The next unit begins formal technical practice.",

    // ---------- m07 保护站基础 ----------
    "lesson.m07.brief.1" to "An anchor connects the person to the mountain, giving a fall force somewhere to go. Without an anchor, a rope is just a loop.",
    "lesson.m07.brief.2" to "There are three families of placement: natural (rock horn, tree, boulder), snow (picket, deadman, T-slot), and ice (ice screw). The medium changes the criteria completely.",
    "lesson.m07.brief.3" to "Evaluate an anchor on four points: solid, redundant, equalized, and no extension. The last three all answer one question — what if one piece fails?",
    "lesson.m07.brief.4" to "The angle between placements multiplies the load: at 0° each leg carries about 50%, at 60° about 58%, at 90° about 71%. The wider the angle, the heavier the load on each piece.",
    "lesson.m07.brief.5" to "In one sentence: if any single piece fails, the system must not collapse. That is what redundancy means.",

    // ---------- m08 目标山峰：岗什卡与哈巴 ----------
    "lesson.m08.brief.1" to "Gangshika lies in Menyuan, Qinghai, and is the highest peak in the eastern Qilian range. The main summit is 5,254.5 m, with a 5,150 m shoulder and a 5,005 m third peak — a natural progression from third peak to main summit.",
    "lesson.m08.brief.2" to "Gangshika III is entry-level technical: about 10 km of approach, roughly 6 hours, with a beginner success rate near 95%. It suits a first encounter with technical gear and ice-slope travel.",
    "lesson.m08.brief.3" to "Haba Snow Mountain, 5,396 m (Shangri-La, Yunnan): ice slopes above the snowline require crampons and axe — a common step from walking to technical terrain.",
    "lesson.m08.brief.4" to "Elbrus, 5,642 m (Caucasus, Russia): the highest in Europe. Crampons and axe are required, and glacier travel in rope teams is a basic expectation.",
    "lesson.m08.brief.5" to "Moving from walking to technical ground, what you mainly need is not fitness but gear handling, arrest ability, and the judgement of when to turn around.",

    // ---------- m09 高原用药与急救药物 ----------
    "lesson.m09.brief.1" to "Altitude drugs have only two goals: speed up acclimatization (acetazolamide) and buy time for descent (dexamethasone, nifedipine). Confusing the two leads to the wrong drug and a misread of the illness.",
    "lesson.m09.brief.2" to "Acetazolamide speeds acclimatization. The usual recommendation is 125 mg twice daily, starting about one day before ascent and stopping about two days after; stopping does not cause a rebound of symptoms.",
    "lesson.m09.brief.3" to "Dexamethasone is a potent steroid that lowers intracranial pressure and relieves cerebral oedema; it is commonly used for HACE. 4 mg every 6 hours. It has no effect on acclimatization and does not treat pulmonary oedema. If vomiting is persistent, it must be given by injection.",
    "lesson.m09.brief.4" to "Nifedipine relieves hypoxia-induced pulmonary vasoconstriction and is used to prevent and treat pulmonary oedema. The usual recommendation is 20–30 mg slow release every 12 hours; it can drop blood pressure sharply, and standing up quickly from sitting or lying may cause fainting.",
    "lesson.m09.brief.5" to "The most important point: drugs relieve symptoms, they do not improve acclimatization. Even if symptoms ease on dexamethasone, do not keep ascending — descend as soon as possible. All medication must follow a doctor's advice.",

    // ---------- m10 山地气象与天气窗口 ----------
    "lesson.m10.brief.1" to "A weather window is the period in which the objective can be completed safely. It sets your departure time, your pace, and your turnaround time.",
    "lesson.m10.brief.2" to "Alpine weather has three traits: it changes fast, varies locally, and is only partly predictable. A regional forecast gives the background trend; for a specific ridge or col you still have to read it yourself.",
    "lesson.m10.brief.3" to "Read trends rather than numbers: falling pressure, thickening and lowering cloud, and strengthening wind — when all three appear together, the weather is almost certainly deteriorating.",
    "lesson.m10.brief.4" to "Wind is the most direct operational constraint. The Beaufort scale converts wind speed into a perceptible grade — it tells you at which grade you can still move.",
    "lesson.m10.brief.5" to "There is also a daily pattern: mornings are usually most stable, while afternoon convection builds cloud, thunderstorms and precipitation — which is why summit pushes start before dawn.",

    // ---------- m11 目标山峰：6000 米四座 ----------
    "lesson.m11.brief.1" to "Yuzhu Peak, 6,178 m (Golmud, Qinghai; eastern Kunlun): the south route is relatively moderate and access is good — a common first 6,000 m objective in China.",
    "lesson.m11.brief.2" to "Qizi Peak, 6,206 m (Damxung, Tibet; Nyainqêntanglha range): stable snow slopes and convenient access make it a good progression peak.",
    "lesson.m11.brief.3" to "Lobuche East, 6,119 m (Khumbu, Nepal): right beside the Everest trekking route, with steeper snow slopes — often used as a bridge between Island Peak and 8,000 m.",
    "lesson.m11.brief.4" to "Island Peak (Imja Tse), 6,189 m (Khumbu, Nepal): requires roped travel and crevasse navigation; a classic technical 6,000 m peak.",
    "lesson.m11.brief.5" to "At 6,000 m the barrier is acclimatization, not technique. Altitude illness risk rises clearly, and crevasses and avalanches can no longer be treated as possible — assume they are present.",

    // ---------- m12 进阶绳索系统与机械优势 ----------
    "lesson.m12.brief.1" to "Basic knots solve connection. Advanced systems solve two harder problems: multiplying force and working under load.",
    "lesson.m12.brief.2" to "Mechanical advantage: the 3:1 Z-system uses a friction hitch plus one pulley to gain three times the pull, and is the standard glacier rescue system. Without a pulley a locking carabiner works, but friction losses rise and efficiency drops.",
    "lesson.m12.brief.3" to "Rescue kit (textbook list): 2–3 snow or ice anchors, 2–5 locking carabiners, 2–5 slings, 1 braking pulley, 2–3 friction hitches, 1 handled ascender.",
    "lesson.m12.brief.4" to "An ascender grips by reverse friction between its teeth and the rope, so excessive force can damage the rope; use it with a backup braking method wherever possible.",
    "lesson.m12.brief.5" to "A system lets one person do what they otherwise could not — but only on the basis of familiarity and repeated drills. Learning a system at the scene of an accident is not learning, it is improvising.",

    // ---------- m13 自救与结组救援 ----------
    "lesson.m13.brief.1" to "Self-rescue is the hard threshold at 7,000 m: your partner may not reach you in time, so you must be able to manage your own situation.",
    "lesson.m13.brief.2" to "Prusik self-ascent is the basic means of holding position or escaping after a crevasse fall: two hitches used alternately, one loaded while the other is moved up.",
    "lesson.m13.brief.3" to "The four steps of roped rescue (arrest → build anchor → transfer load → haul) were learned at 6,000 m; here the goal is to execute them under pressure.",
    "lesson.m13.brief.4" to "Estimating lightning distance: the seconds between flash and thunder × 0.34 = distance in kilometres. Sound travels at about 0.34 km/s in air.",
    "lesson.m13.brief.5" to "If the team meets a thunderstorm, keep roughly 50 m apart so a single strike cannot injure several people; small metal items do not attract lightning, so do not waste time throwing them away.",

    // ---------- m14 危险判断与决策 ----------
    "lesson.m14.brief.1" to "The core question is not whether there is danger, but whether the risk is acceptable — and that requires turning a subjective feeling into something discussable and shareable.",
    "lesson.m14.brief.2" to "The accident chain: serious accidents usually result from several small errors stacking up (optimistic judgement → running late → simplified protection → fatigue error). Break any link and the accident does not happen.",
    "lesson.m14.brief.3" to "Decision biases ignore experience level: sunk cost (we have invested too much), team pressure (not wanting to hold others back), goal fixation (we are nearly there), and risk normalisation (it was fine last time).",
    "lesson.m14.brief.4" to "In high-risk settings the most conservative view should take precedence. Majority voting suppresses the reasonable concern of the few — and the line most often heard in accident reviews is \"someone felt uneasy, but nobody said so\".",
    "lesson.m14.brief.5" to "A concrete countermeasure: quantify the criteria before departure (turnaround time, weather limits, condition limits), so that relaxing them becomes an explicit act you must explain to your team.",

    // ---------- m15 天气识别与云图判读 ----------
    "lesson.m15.brief.1" to "Reading clouds buys you lead time: cloud is the most visible early sign of change, and you see it before the barometer registers it.",
    "lesson.m15.brief.2" to "Cumulus → cumulonimbus is the classic thunderstorm path. Vertical growth shows strong convection; once an anvil top appears, the storm is mature and lightning, hail and heavy rain are all possible.",
    "lesson.m15.brief.3" to "Cumulus usually forms in the afternoon, as daytime heating drives convection. Starting early and descending in good time is the most basic defence against thunderstorms.",
    "lesson.m15.brief.4" to "Cloud types carry different meanings: lenticular clouds often accompany strong wind; increasing cirrus (wispy, high) signals an approaching system; lowering stratus warns of deteriorating visibility.",
    "lesson.m15.brief.5" to "Cloud reading must be combined with pressure trend: clouds tell you what is happening now, pressure tells you where it is heading.",

    // ---------- m16 雪崩风险判断（进阶） ----------
    "lesson.m16.brief.1" to "Advanced assessment stacks three scales: the regional forecast (background risk), the terrain (whether snow can accumulate and slide), and the snowpack (stability on the spot). Missing any one leads to error.",
    "lesson.m16.brief.2" to "Slope angle is the strongest single predictor: about 96% of avalanches occur on 30–50° slopes, most between 34 and 45°. Below 30° is too gentle; above 50° snow rarely accumulates.",
    "lesson.m16.brief.3" to "Accidents cluster at danger level 3 (considerable) — because conditions look reasonable and are therefore most easily underestimated.",
    "lesson.m16.brief.4" to "Field signals: whumpfing collapse in the snowpack, fresh cracks across the slope, cornices and wind slabs built by strong wind.",
    "lesson.m16.brief.5" to "Team tactics: spread out, cross one at a time, post a watcher in a safe spot, and plan the escape route in advance.",

    // ---------- m17 高海拔营地与物资管理 ----------
    "lesson.m17.brief.1" to "Camp management has one goal: sustain working capacity at the lowest cost. Warmth, sleep, food and water — if any one fails, next day's performance drops sharply.",
    "lesson.m17.brief.2" to "Site selection: avoid avalanche paths and icefall zones, avoid ground below cornices, avoid crevasses, and choose a sheltered, level spot.",
    "lesson.m17.brief.3" to "Split fuel, food and oxygen by day and keep an emergency reserve. Saving one portion is not conservatism; it guarantees you can descend safely under any scenario.",
    "lesson.m17.brief.4" to "Gear discipline: wet clothes into the sleeping bag, inner boots dried, electronics kept warm overnight. These three small habits decide how your hands, feet and batteries perform tomorrow.",
    "lesson.m17.brief.5" to "Evacuation plan: camp is the pivot for retreat, so keep it in a state where you could pack and leave immediately.",

    // ---------- m18 目标山峰：慕士塔格与阿玛达布朗 ----------
    "lesson.m18.brief.1" to "Muztagh Ata, 7,509 m (Akto, Xinjiang): the \"father of the ice mountains\". Long snow slopes and moderate technical difficulty, but high demands on acclimatization and fitness — the usual first 7,000 m choice in China.",
    "lesson.m18.brief.2" to "Ama Dablam, 6,812 m (Khumbu, Nepal): a 6,000 m altitude but mixed rock-and-snow terrain with strong exposure gives it technical difficulty comparable to 7,000 m — often used as a test piece for 8,000 m.",
    "lesson.m18.brief.3" to "Lenin Peak, 7,134 m (Pamir, Kyrgyz–Tajik border): one of the more moderate 7,000 m options, often taken as a first 7,000 m.",
    "lesson.m18.brief.4" to "Himlung Himal, 7,126 m (Nepal): mixed snow slopes and ice walls; a popular 7,000 m objective in Nepal.",
    "lesson.m18.brief.5" to "7,000 m is a watershed: acclimatization cycles lengthen, weather windows narrow, and retreat becomes harder. Technique is no longer the only threshold — judgement, fitness and logistics all start to count together.",

    // ---------- m19 8000 米生理极限与缺氧 ----------
    "lesson.m19.brief.1" to "Above 8,000 m is the \"death zone\": atmospheric pressure is about one third of sea level, so each breath brings in less than a third of the oxygen molecules.",
    "lesson.m19.brief.2" to "At that altitude the body cannot stay long — even at rest it is steadily declining (weight loss, muscle loss, cognitive decline).",
    "lesson.m19.brief.3" to "Judgement usually fails first: hypoxia attacks higher brain functions (calculation, weighing options, risk recognition) while physical decline lags behind.",
    "lesson.m19.brief.4" to "That yields a hard rule: at 8,000 m do not trust how you feel on the spot; rely on checklists and rules written beforehand.",
    "lesson.m19.brief.5" to "The risks at 8,000 m are not only altitude illness but avalanches, falls and hypothermia. Very often the real root cause is descending too late.",

    // ---------- m20 氧气系统与补给 ----------
    "lesson.m20.brief.1" to "Supplemental oxygen raises blood oxygen, lowers altitude-illness risk, and improves performance and judgement. At 8,000 m it is standard safety equipment, not cheating.",
    "lesson.m20.brief.2" to "The system consists of cylinder, regulator, flow meter, and mask or nasal cannula. Climbing flows are commonly 2–4 L/min; how long a cylinder lasts depends on its capacity and the flow rate.",
    "lesson.m20.brief.3" to "The core discipline is arithmetic: calculate ascent, descent and emergency reserve separately, rather than only asking whether there is enough to get up.",
    "lesson.m20.brief.4" to "Running out is very dangerous: the body has come to depend on enriched oxygen, and a sudden return to ambient partial pressure can cause rapid deterioration — so the oxygen plan must carry a safety margin.",
    "lesson.m20.brief.5" to "Oxygen does not change the environment, does not shorten the route, and does not replace acclimatization. It only raises the physiological ceiling temporarily.",

    // ---------- m21 世界 14 座 8000 米（上） ----------
    "lesson.m21.brief.1" to "There are 14 peaks above 8,000 m worldwide, all in the Himalaya and Karakoram, spanning China, Nepal, India and Pakistan.",
    "lesson.m21.brief.2" to "This lesson covers the ten in the Himalaya: Everest, Kangchenjunga, Lhotse, Makalu, Cho Oyu, Nanga Parbat, Dhaulagiri, Manaslu, Annapurna and Shishapangma.",
    "lesson.m21.brief.3" to "Everest, 8,848 m: the world's highest and the most commercially developed — but developed does not mean low risk.",
    "lesson.m21.brief.4" to "Annapurna I, 8,091 m: the first 8,000 m peak to be climbed (1950) and long among the deadliest — first does not mean easy.",
    "lesson.m21.brief.5" to "Shishapangma, 8,027 m: the only 8,000 m peak entirely within China (Tibet), and the last of the fourteen to be climbed (1964, Chinese team).",

    // ---------- m22 世界 14 座 8000 米（下） ----------
    "lesson.m22.brief.1" to "The Karakoram holds four 8,000 m peaks: K2, Gasherbrum I, Gasherbrum II and Broad Peak — all near the China–Pakistan border.",
    "lesson.m22.brief.2" to "K2, 8,611 m: the second highest in the world and widely regarded as one of the most technical and deadliest 8,000 m peaks — the climber's mountain.",
    "lesson.m22.brief.3" to "Broad Peak, 8,051 m, also called K3: relatively open terrain but very heavy snowfall, and once underestimated for being \"easy\".",
    "lesson.m22.brief.4" to "The biggest difference between Karakoram and Himalaya is climate: the Karakoram is more strongly influenced by the westerlies, with weather windows and precipitation patterns quite unlike the Everest region.",
    "lesson.m22.brief.5" to "Seen together, the fourteen lead to one key conclusion: difficulty comes mainly from technical terrain, climate and logistics — not from the altitude ranking.",

    // ---------- m23 终极综合考核（上） ----------
    "lesson.m23.brief.1" to "This assessment covers the main line: environment, gear, clothing, movement and ice-axe arrest.",
    "lesson.m23.brief.2" to "The point is not reciting facts but assembling them: given a scenario, you should be able to move from environment to gear choice to technical action.",
    "lesson.m23.brief.3" to "Hint: when unsure, return to the root causal chain — low pressure → low oxygen partial pressure → acclimatization takes time. Many questions can be derived from there.",

    // ---------- m24 终极综合考核（下） ----------
    "lesson.m24.brief.1" to "This assessment covers ropes and protection, crevasses, altitude medicine, avalanches, and decision-making in rescue.",
    "lesson.m24.brief.2" to "These topics share one trait: they are used only after something has gone wrong. So the assessment is not whether you know them, but whether you can execute under pressure.",
    "lesson.m24.brief.3" to "Finishing this completes the route from 5,000 m foundations to the 8,000 m final assessment — but remember: theoretical knowledge is not field ability, and every technical skill still needs on-site instructor evaluation.",

    // ---------- m25 高原心理与决策习惯 ----------
    "lesson.m25.brief.1" to "At altitude two things matter more than fitness: telling the truth and not pushing on. This is not an attitude problem — hypoxia hits judgement first, so \"I think I'm fine\" is discounted at altitude.",
    "lesson.m25.brief.2" to "\"We've come all this way\" is the most expensive sentence in mountaineering: it turns time already invested into a reason to keep taking risk. The mountain does not lower its risk because of what you have spent.",
    "lesson.m25.brief.3" to "When your body signals, the right first step is not to tough it out but to stop, observe, and tell your partner or guide honestly. A guide can only judge what they know.",
    "lesson.m25.brief.4" to "Slow is fast: a sustainable steady pace saves more time and is safer than repeated sprints — this describes energy metabolism, not attitude.",
    "lesson.m25.brief.5" to "Say disagreement out loud. The silent minority is a regular in accident reviews; holding an opinion is not the same as refusing to execute, and both can happen at once.",

    // ---------- l21 穿着系统与分层 ----------
    "lesson.l21.brief.1" to "The three layers each have a job: base layer manages sweat, mid layer insulates, shell blocks wind and rain. No layer substitutes for another; mixing them up serves neither purpose.",
    "lesson.l21.brief.2" to "Never use cotton next to the skin: it holds water and barely wicks, so damp cold stays against the body and keeps drawing heat away. Choose polyester or merino wool that moves moisture fast.",
    "lesson.l21.brief.3" to "Fleece is made of polyester, naturally hydrophobic, and still insulates when damp — which is why it is more reliable than down in wet conditions, and why it is the usual mid layer.",
    "lesson.l21.brief.4" to "Down jackets should be 800 fill power or above; for extreme cold choose a hooded, non-synthetic-fill model. Down is highly efficient but loses almost all insulation once wet.",
    "lesson.l21.brief.5" to "The shell (Gore-Tex jacket and trousers) handles waterproofing, windproofing and breathability, and must be helmet-compatible. Gaiters keep snow out of boots; long models are recommended at altitude.",

    // ---------- l22 技术器材与导航通讯 ----------
    "lesson.l22.brief.1" to "Ascent and protection kit: handled ascender, locking carabiners, non-locking carabiners, figure-8 or ATC tube belay device, and a 120 cm sling.",
    "lesson.l22.brief.2" to "The prusik loop (cord) is the basis of crevasse rescue: it makes the friction hitch that is central to a 3:1 mechanical advantage system, and must be carried at all times.",
    "lesson.l22.brief.3" to "Navigation and communication: altimeter watch (with barometer and storm alarm), GPS tracker, satellite phone. There is no mobile signal in the mountains; satellite communication is often the only way to call for help.",
    "lesson.l22.brief.4" to "Lighting: the headtorch must fit on a helmet; a backup should be 400 lumens or more (500-lumen models are clearly better in bad weather), and weatherproof models are preferable.",
    "lesson.l22.brief.5" to "The harness must still fit over your outermost clothing; choose large locking carabiners so they can be operated with gloves on.",

    // ---------- l1 高山环境与风险认知 ----------
    "lesson.l1.brief.1" to "Alpine danger comes in two kinds. Objective danger does not change with your skill: rockfall, icefall, avalanches, sudden weather, crevasses. Subjective danger is yours to control: misjudgement, poor preparation, exhaustion, comparison with others, summit fixation.",
    "lesson.l1.brief.2" to "Training can reduce subjective danger. Objective danger cannot be removed — only avoided, timed well, and given a way out.",
    "lesson.l1.brief.3" to "Temperature falls with altitude, about 6.5 °C per 1,000 m; pressure and oxygen partial pressure fall with it, which is the root of altitude illness.",

    // ---------- l2 装备认知：冰镐与冰爪 ----------
    "lesson.l2.brief.1" to "Ice axes fall into two types. Type B (basic) has a straighter shaft for snow-slope travel and self-arrest; Type T (technical) has a shaft that can be placed in ice and loaded, for steep ice and technical climbing.",
    "lesson.l2.brief.2" to "Crampons come in three grades: C1 full strap, C2 strap-and-clip, C3 full clip. The higher the grade, the stiffer the setup and the more demanding the boot.",
    "lesson.l2.brief.3" to "Boots and crampons must match: B1 soft boots with C1, B2 semi-rigid with C2, B3 rigid with C3. This pairing is a safety baseline, not a preference.",

    // ---------- l3 高山靴与穿戴系统 ----------
    "lesson.l3.brief.1" to "Single boots are light and nimble, suited to lower altitudes and ice climbing; double boots (with removable inner) insulate better and can be dried out, suited to extreme cold and multi-day high-altitude trips.",
    "lesson.l3.brief.2" to "Mountaineering boots must match the crampons and must not let the toes jam the front. On descents the foot slides forward, and an undersized boot damages toenails and toe bones.",
    "lesson.l3.brief.3" to "Snow goggles are essential, not an accessory: snow reflects UV strongly and can cause snow blindness and severe sunburn. Choose UV400 protection.",
    "lesson.l3.brief.4" to "Carry two pairs of gloves (thick for warmth, thin for dexterity); the insulation layer must be waterproof, and spare clothing must be packed sealed against moisture.",

    // ---------- l4 雪坡行走基础 ----------
    "lesson.l4.brief.1" to "Kick steps: cut steps with the edge of the boot in firm snow — short strides, even rhythm, stable centre of gravity. The flatter you cut them, the less effort for everyone behind.",
    "lesson.l4.brief.2" to "Hold the axe in the uphill hand as a cane (piolet canne) for support — so that if you lose balance you can switch straight into arrest without changing hands.",
    "lesson.l4.brief.3" to "On the ascent use pressure breathing with your steps: deep inhale, forceful exhale, synchronised with cadence; it noticeably relieves the suffocating feeling at altitude.",
    "lesson.l4.brief.4" to "Always keep at least two stable points of contact; when turning, plant yourself first and then turn — do not twist while moving.",

    // ---------- l5 冰爪技术 ----------
    "lesson.l5.brief.1" to "French technique (flat-footing): all points of the crampon contact the slope with the foot flat, without kicking the front points. Most efficient, suited to low to moderate angles and firm snow.",
    "lesson.l5.brief.2" to "German technique (front-pointing): the front points are kicked vertically into the ice and the calves do the work. Suited to slopes above 45° and ice walls, but the most demanding physically.",
    "lesson.l5.brief.3" to "Mixed (American) technique: one foot flat on the slope for support while the other drives with the front points, alternating — used on transition terrain from moderate to steep.",
    "lesson.l5.brief.4" to "When traversing a snow slope, use the duck step: heels together, toes out, so the whole crampon sits on the slope instead of hanging on the inner points.",

    // ---------- l6 冰镐使用与自我制动 ----------
    "lesson.l6.brief.1" to "Arrest position: roll quickly face-down into the slope, hold the axe diagonally across the chest, one hand over the head (near the pick) and the other on the shaft.",
    "lesson.l6.brief.2" to "Once the pick is in the snow, drive it deeper with your body weight — the more weight, the deeper it bites.",
    "lesson.l6.brief.3" to "Another common mistake: keep both feet off the snow, toes up. The moment your feet touch, the weight on the axe disappears.",
    "lesson.l6.brief.4" to "If you are sliding head-first, the first thing is to roll face-down with your feet downhill, and only then establish the arrest.",
    "lesson.l6.brief.5" to "Self-arrest is pure muscle memory. It must be drilled repeatedly on a soft-snow slope with an instructor belaying; video and books cannot build an effective reflex.",

    // ---------- l7 滑坠应对与制动反应 ----------
    "lesson.l7.brief.1" to "First principle: do not fall. Keep using self-belay as you move — plant the axe shaft in the snow as a temporary point, so every step has support.",
    "lesson.l7.brief.2" to "If you lose balance: shout \"Falling!\" to warn the team → roll face-down → establish the arrest. The three happen almost simultaneously.",
    "lesson.l7.brief.3" to "If a partner falls, first get yourself into arrest position and take in rope. Do not reach out to grab them — that usually takes both of you down.",
    "lesson.l7.brief.4" to "Arrest decelerates, it does not stop instantly. On steep firm snow the braking distance can be a dozen metres or more, so act early.",

    // ---------- l8 雪坡下降与滑降 ----------
    "lesson.l8.brief.1" to "Descent is often more dangerous than ascent: energy is spent, attention drops, and the body leans forward naturally — many accidents happen on the way down.",
    "lesson.l8.brief.2" to "On technical ground, prefer facing-in downclimbing: face the slope, three points of contact, one step at a time, with vision ahead and a stable centre of gravity.",
    "lesson.l8.brief.3" to "Controlled glissading is only acceptable where there are no crevasses, no rockfall risk, and an open slope with nobody below — and you must be able to arrest at any moment.",
    "lesson.l8.brief.4" to "While glissading, keep a hand on the axe head so it stays ready to arrest — never stow the axe in the pack or let it swing to the other side of the body.",

    // ---------- l9 基础绳结 ----------
    "lesson.l9.brief.1" to "Figure-8 follow-through: the most common knot for tying into the harness. Easy to inspect visually and still unties after loading; the standard tie-in for glacier and alpine routes.",
    "lesson.l9.brief.2" to "Clove hitch: attaches the rope to a carabiner and allows one-handed length adjustment; the standard knot for securing yourself to an anchor.",
    "lesson.l9.brief.3" to "Double fisherman's: joins two ropes, or makes a prusik loop.",
    "lesson.l9.brief.4" to "Prusik: a friction hitch that grips under load and slides when unloaded — used for ascending, backup and rescue systems.",
    "lesson.l9.brief.5" to "Leave enough tail after tying, generally at least ten rope diameters, and then check each other's knot. How well it is tied directly affects safety.",

    // ---------- l10 安全带与结组系统 ----------
    "lesson.l10.brief.1" to "On a glacier, three to a rope is ideal: if one falls into a crevasse, the other two still have margin to build protection and rescue. A two-person rope has little margin and clearly higher risk.",
    "lesson.l10.brief.2" to "Rope spacing is usually 8–12 m. Knots between the rope sections (such as an alpine butterfly) add friction where the rope cuts into snow and slow a falling climber.",
    "lesson.l10.brief.3" to "A rope team is not a guarantee. It works only if your partners can arrest effectively and build protection. If they cannot, the rope team becomes a collective fall.",
    "lesson.l10.brief.4" to "Wear the harness throughout glacier travel, and carry carabiners, prusik loops and snow pickets within reach at all times.",

    // ---------- l11 保护站原则 ----------
    "lesson.l11.brief.1" to "SRENE: Solid, Redundant, Equalized, No Extension.",
    "lesson.l11.brief.2" to "EARNEST is a fuller framework: Efficient, Anchored, Redundant, No Extension, Strong, Timely.",
    "lesson.l11.brief.3" to "The test is simple: remove any one piece — does the system still hold? Only then is it redundant.",
    "lesson.l11.brief.4" to "Common placements: snow pickets, deadmen and T-slots in snow; ice screws in ice. Different media need different techniques.",

    // ---------- l12 冰裂缝与结组行进 ----------
    "lesson.l12.brief.1" to "Crevasses are the main hazard on a glacier. Snow bridges can be completely covered by fresh snow and look identical to ordinary snow — that is what makes them so deadly.",
    "lesson.l12.brief.2" to "Cross crevasse fields roped up, with the rope moderately taut and without large loops hanging down; otherwise the faller drops free before the rope comes tight.",
    "lesson.l12.brief.3" to "Standard sequence when someone falls in: partners arrest immediately → build a snow anchor → transfer the load to the anchor → haul with a mechanical advantage system.",
    "lesson.l12.brief.4" to "The most common hauling system is the 3:1 Z-system (Z-pulley / Z-drag): a friction hitch and pulley on the loaded rope, combined with the anchor to give three times the advantage.",
    "lesson.l12.brief.5" to "The fallen climber should stay calm, try prusik self-ascent, and avoid letting the rope saw further into the snow lip at the crevasse edge.",

    // ---------- l13 急性高原病 ----------
    "lesson.l13.brief.1" to "Diagnostic criteria (Lake Louise): after a recent ascent to altitude, headache plus at least one of nausea or vomiting, dizziness, fatigue or weakness, or sleep disturbance.",
    "lesson.l13.brief.2" to "Timing: usually 6–12 hours after arriving at altitude. Incidence rises with altitude — data suggest about 25–43% between 2,500 and 4,300 m, and it is uncommon below 2,500 m.",
    "lesson.l13.brief.3" to "Golden rule: if symptoms appear, do not ascend further; if they worsen, descend immediately. This is the one judgement that is never wrong.",
    "lesson.l13.brief.4" to "Understand medication carefully: painkillers ease headache, but relief is not acclimatization — treating \"it no longer hurts\" as \"we can keep going up\" is a typical and fatal misjudgement.",

    // ---------- l14 HACE 与 HAPE ----------
    "lesson.l14.brief.1" to "HACE (high-altitude cerebral oedema): altered consciousness, unsteady gait (failing the heel-to-toe walk), drowsiness, abnormal behaviour. It is a medical emergency and can kill within hours.",
    "lesson.l14.brief.2" to "HAPE (high-altitude pulmonary oedema): breathlessness at rest, dry cough turning productive with frothy sputum, blue lips, sharply reduced capacity. HAPE is the leading cause of death from altitude illness.",
    "lesson.l14.brief.3" to "The fundamental treatment for both is immediate descent. Supporting measures: oxygen, a hyperbaric bag (Gamow bag), and drug intervention.",
    "lesson.l14.brief.4" to "Common drugs (must be guided by a doctor): dexamethasone for HACE and moderate-to-severe AMS, typically 4 mg every 6 hours in adults; nifedipine or a phosphodiesterase inhibitor for HAPE. Drugs buy time; they do not replace descent.",

    // ---------- l15 高原适应策略 ----------
    "lesson.l15.brief.1" to "Core pacing: avoid sleeping directly above 2,750 m in a single day; above 3,000 m keep the daily gain in sleeping altitude under 500 m, and take roughly one rest day per 1,000 m gained.",
    "lesson.l15.brief.2" to "Climb high, sleep low: go higher during the day to work and acclimatize, then return to a lower altitude to sleep. This acclimatizes more efficiently than staying high all the time.",
    "lesson.l15.brief.3" to "Acetazolamide is used to prevent altitude illness, usually starting the day before ascent, 125 mg twice daily in adults. It is a prescription drug and must follow medical advice.",
    "lesson.l15.brief.4" to "Non-drug measures work too: drink enough, eat mainly carbohydrate, avoid alcohol and sedatives, and protect sleep quality.",

    // ---------- l16 失温与冻伤 ----------
    "lesson.l16.brief.1" to "Stages of hypothermia: mild (shivering, alert) → moderate (shivering stops, confusion, slurred speech) → severe (unconscious, weak pulse and breathing).",
    "lesson.l16.brief.2" to "The easiest thing to misread: shivering stopping is not improvement, it is deterioration. Many people get this exactly backwards.",
    "lesson.l16.brief.3" to "For moderate hypothermia and worse, do not move the patient roughly, and do not let the limbs exercise hard — the cold heart is easily triggered into arrhythmia. Rewarm the trunk first, not the hands and feet.",
    "lesson.l16.brief.4" to "Rewarming frostbite: immerse in water at 37–40 °C for about 30 minutes until the skin reddens and softens. Never rub with snow, never hold to a fire, never apply a hot-water bottle directly.",
    "lesson.l16.brief.5" to "If there is a risk of refreezing, do not rewarm in the field — damage from refreezing is far worse than a single freeze. Descend with the injury. Do not burst blisters.",

    // ---------- l17 攀冰技术基础 ----------
    "lesson.l17.brief.1" to "Swing with the wrist, not with the whole arm. The pick enters vertically, and a crisp \"thunk\" means good placement; a dull sound usually means a poor angle or rotten ice.",
    "lesson.l17.brief.2" to "Kick: raise the foot with a bent knee and drive the front points straight in. Stabilise both feet before swinging, keeping the three-point rhythm — move the fourth point only when three are secure.",
    "lesson.l17.brief.3" to "WI water-ice grades: WI1 low angle, walkable; WI2 about 60° with bulges and good protection; WI3 sustained 60–75°, physically demanding; WI4 near vertical with short vertical sections, protection possibly poor.",
    "lesson.l17.brief.4" to "WI5 is sustained vertical with poor and strenuous protection; WI6 is sustained vertical with overhangs and very poor protection. The higher the grade, the less room for retreat and self-rescue.",
    "lesson.l17.brief.5" to "Ice climbing depends heavily on ice conditions and personal condition, and must be belayed from below by someone experienced. Ice changes daily; yesterday's grade may be completely different today.",

    // ---------- l18 雪崩基础 ----------
    "lesson.l18.brief.1" to "Slope angle is the strongest single predictor: about 96% of avalanches occur on 30–50° slopes, most between 34 and 45°.",
    "lesson.l18.brief.2" to "Below about 30° the slope is too gentle to produce slab avalanches; above about 50° snow does not accumulate readily, so thick slabs are less likely. The slope that \"doesn't look that steep\" is the most dangerous.",
    "lesson.l18.brief.3" to "Danger levels 1–5: low / moderate / considerable / high / extreme. Statistically, accidents cluster at level 3, considerable — because terrain looks reasonable and is most easily underestimated.",
    "lesson.l18.brief.4" to "The avalanche trio must be carried and usable: transceiver, probe, shovel. Without equipment, burial survival is measured in about fifteen minutes.",
    "lesson.l18.brief.5" to "When crossing avalanche terrain, spread out, cross one at a time, post a watcher, and plan the escape route — so the whole team is never buried together.",

    // ---------- l19 攀登计划与撤退决策 ----------
    "lesson.l19.brief.1" to "Set a turnaround time: when it arrives, you turn around, however close the summit is. This is the most effective hard rule in mountaineering.",
    "lesson.l19.brief.2" to "The summit is only half the journey. Many accidents happen on the descent, because alertness drops once the goal is reached while energy is already near its limit.",
    "lesson.l19.brief.3" to "Summit fever systematically distorts judgement: sunk cost, team pressure, goal fixation, and the unwillingness to give up \"after coming this far\". It does not spare experience — guides and veterans fall for it too.",
    "lesson.l19.brief.4" to "Agree the retreat decision in advance: who has the authority to call it? What objective signals trigger it (time, weather window, team condition, snow conditions)? Settle it beforehand so emotions cannot hijack the decision on the spot.",

    // ---------- l20 应急与救援流程 ----------
    "lesson.l20.brief.1" to "Emergency sequence: make the scene safe → assess the casualty (airway, breathing, circulation, consciousness) → call for help → basic care (insulation, bleeding control, splinting) → move or wait for professional rescue.",
    "lesson.l20.brief.2" to "The first principle is: do not create a second casualty. Every rescue action must happen only with you and the rest of the team safe.",
    "lesson.l20.brief.3" to "High-altitude rescue is tightly constrained by weather, oxygen, communication and manpower, and often takes hours to days. Precisely because rescue is so hard, prevention is worth far more than rescue.",
    "lesson.l20.brief.4" to "Leave a detailed itinerary and contact person before departure, and know the local rescue resources and communication options (satellite devices, radios, emergency frequencies). Without communication there is no call for help."
)
